  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA cool high pressure system will persist across the region\nthrough Saturday, allowing for dry weather for the next 36-48\nhours. Temperatures will continue to moderate upward through the\nperiod with highs near normal in the 80s. Low temperatures will\nremain cooler with 50s tonight and some isolated 40s in\nsheltered areas of NW PA and NE OH. Record low temperatures are\npossible tonight, especially if temperatures can creep into the\n40s. Lows will slowly increase each night through the weekend.\n\nSome isentropic lift and added moisture into the region on\nSaturday may allow for some isolated showers and storms to pop\nup in far NE OH and NW PA and have some new low PoPs to account\nfor that. Overall, most of the area should be dry on Saturday,\nallowing for a dry narrative to prevail for most.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn upper trough over the Hudson Bay of Canada will drop south a\nbit and allow for northwest flow aloft to setup across the area.\nMeanwhile, mid-to-upper 60s dew points will return to the region\nin this northwest flow regime. This should allow for convective\nactivity to pick up across the Great Lakes region on Sunday\nthrough Tuesday. Current guidance suggests that storms will be\nmore organized to the west on Sunday and eventually make it into\nthe area late. Whereas, the remnant boundaries of convection\nwill be over the region on Monday and Tuesday to allow for more\ncoverage of storms in the region. With a moist and conditionally\nunstable air mass on the northeast side of a ridge, there will\nbe concerns for strong to severe storms, and the area remains\noutlooked in a Day 5 SPC Severe Risk area.",
